  <h2>Where AI blog drafts read flat</h2>
  <p>Blog drafts from an assistant often open with a line about "today's fast-paced world", promise to "delve into" the topic, and close each section with a tidy restatement. Readers learn to skim that pattern fast. Removing it makes the post shorter and puts your real points closer to the top.</p>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <blockquote><p><strong>Before:</strong> In today's digital age, email marketing plays a vital role in customer retention. Additionally, it serves as a cost-effective channel for a wide range of businesses.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote><p><strong>After:</strong> Today, email marketing is key to customer retention. It is a cost-effective channel for all kinds of businesses.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Your formatting stays put</h2>
  <p>The editor works one paragraph at a time. Headings, tables, code blocks and quoted passages are left exactly as they are, list numbering is locked, and links are locked so an anchor or URL can't change. You can paste Markdown straight from your CMS and paste the result straight back.</p>

  <h2>A publishing routine that works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Run the post at Balanced, then read the edit log rather than the whole text.</li>
    <li>Undo any edit that shifts a term your readers search for. Revert it with "This edit is wrong".</li>
    <li>Add what no tool can add: your own data, a screenshot, a customer quote or a mistake you made.</li>
    <li>Rewrite the introduction yourself. It sets the voice for everything after it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Our <a href="/guides/edit-ai-blog-post">guide to editing an AI blog post</a> goes through this in more detail.</p>
